  "constraints": "* Input arrays/tensors are non-empty and well-formed\n* Floating point values are finite real numbers\n* Numerical operations must adhere to standard precision tolerances",
  "starterCode": "-- SQL Self Join\nSELECT u.user_id, u.username, COALESCE(r.username, 'Organic') AS referrer_name\nFROM users u\nLEFT JOIN users r ON u.referrer_id = r.user_id\nORDER BY u.user_id;",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **Self Join** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\ndef sql_self_join(users: list) -> list:\n    umap = {u['user_id']: u['username'] for u in users}\n    res = [{'user_id': u['user_id'], 'username': u['username'], 'referrer_name': umap.get(u.get('referrer_id'), 'Organic')} for u in users]\n    res.sort(key=lambda x: x['user_id'])\n    return res\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
